In the FIS Recurring Payment file, the key field used to distinguish the recurring **MODEL** from an actual payment **INSTANCE** is `Recurring/Single Payment Identifier` **(Position 573-574, Length 2)**. A blank/null value represents the recurring **MODEL**, while a populated value, typically `1`, represents an actual scheduled payment **INSTANCE** generated from that model.

The main recurring payment fields are:

`Payment Amount` **(Position 291-302)**
Amount of each payment.

`Payment Schedule Date` **(Position 303-310)**
For the model, this is the starting/anchor date of the recurring instruction. For an instance, this is the actual scheduled processing date for that specific payment.

`Payment Due Date` **(Position 311-318)**
The date the payment is intended to be due/delivered.

`Payment End Date` **(Position 319-326)**
The date through which the recurring instruction remains active.

`Payment Frequency` **(Position 327-332)**
Defines how often the payment repeats and is mainly meaningful on the model.

`Recurring/Single Payment Identifier` **(Position 573-574)**
Blank/null = MODEL
`1` = INSTANCE

Example:

`MODEL | Amount=19.99 | Schedule=20260825 | Due=20260827 | End=20261125 | Frequency=Monthly | Identifier=blank`

Possible generated instances:

`INSTANCE | Amount=19.99 | Schedule=20260825 | Identifier=1`
`INSTANCE | Amount=19.99 | Schedule=20260925 | Identifier=1`
`INSTANCE | Amount=19.99 | Schedule=20261025 | Identifier=1`

An important edge case is that the extract may contain **only the MODEL and no INSTANCE records**. This does not automatically indicate an error or an inactive recurring payment. The FIS deconversion file is effectively a **snapshot of the data at the time the extract is produced**, and FIS may not have materialized every future recurring occurrence as an individual instance at that point. The recurring model itself still contains the information required to understand the intended future payments through `Schedule Date + Frequency + End Date + Amount`. As FIS processes the recurring instruction, individual instances may be generated later, while previously completed occurrences may appear in payment history instead. Therefore, the **MODEL should be treated as the source of truth for the recurring setup**, and INSTANCE records should be treated as confirmation of specific payment occurrences that had already been generated at the time of the snapshot.
